<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Alaouite Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1631 CE – 1957 CE · capital Meknes</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The sharifian dynasty from Tafilalt that reunited Morocco in the 1660s and has reigned ever since, keeping the country outside Ottoman rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaouite-dynasty">Alaouites</a> were a sharifian family settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a> oasis. Amid the collapse of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulay-ali-al-sharif">Moulay Ali al-Sharif</a> was proclaimed leader there around 1631. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-rashid-of-morocco">Moulay al-Rashid</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in 1666, crushed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dilaiyya">Dila'iyya</a> in 1668 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> in 1669, reuniting <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulay-ismail">Moulay Ismail</a>, ruling from 1672 to 1727, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meknes">Meknes</a> a vast imperial city and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-guard-morocco">Abid al-Bukhari</a>, an army of enslaved black soldiers. He recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehdya">La Mamora</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larache">Larache</a> from Europeans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-iii-of-morocco">Sidi Mohammed ibn Abdallah</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essaouira">Essaouira</a> as a trading port and in 1777 was among the first rulers to recognise the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>. The sultan ruled through the makhzen, with tribes alternately obedient or dissident.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Defeats by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-isly">Isly</a> in 1844 and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1860 opened <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a> to European pressure, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fez">Treaty of Fez</a> of 1912 created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-protectorate-in-morocco">French protectorate in Morocco</a>; a Franco-Spanish treaty later that year set up the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-protectorate-in-morocco">Spanish protectorate in Morocco</a> in the north. The dynasty survived, and after independence in 1956 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-v-of-morocco">Mohammed V</a> took the title of king, forming the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1631 CE – 1957 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.16%;width:6.49%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1631 CE – 1957 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Meknes</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Moulay al-Rashid, Moulay Ismail, Sidi Mohammed ibn Abdallah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Maliki)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Morocco</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Alaouite Morocco, Sultanate of Morocco, Sharifian Empire</dd></dl></aside></div>
